<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

    <!--
      The gateway's suite. docs/plan/10.

      ⚠ NO TestServer, NO WebApplicationFactory — and that is a choice with a reason rather than a
      limitation being worked around. Two suites step outside it, each for one property the shape
      below cannot express, and each says so in its header: HubTicketOverHttpTests starts Kestrel
      on a loopback port because a WebSocket upgrade is decided after the pipeline by Kestrel and
      SignalR, and RedisHubTicketStoreTests starts a Redis container because the store's one claim
      is that two processes see one ticket.

      The gateway's nine stages are composed by GatewayPipeline out of eight IGatewayStage objects
      that take an HttpContext and return an outcome. DefaultHttpContext is in the ASP.NET Core
      shared framework, so the WHOLE pipeline — not a slice of it — runs in-process against a real
      HttpContext with no server, no socket and no Kestrel. What that buys:

        * The tests drive the same object graph production does, in the same order, including the
          response writer. A TestServer would add a socket and change nothing else.
        * `Microsoft.AspNetCore.TestHost` is not in Directory.Packages.props, so this avoids adding
          a package to a central file three other branches are editing right now.

      What it costs, stated so it can be paid: nothing exercises Kestrel's own limits (request body
      size, header count) or the routing table's endpoint matching. Both are framework behaviour
      rather than ours, and the gateway's own body cap is asserted here directly.

      The connection grain is exercised by direct instantiation rather than through a TestCluster,
      which ConnectionGrain's own remarks explain and constrain: it uses no grain infrastructure.

      ⚠ ONE CONTAINER AFTER ALL, FOR ONE SUITE, AND THE "NO CONTAINER" ABOVE STILL HOLDS FOR THE
      PIPELINE. ResourceGraphQueryEndToEndTests (#54) starts a ClickHouse in Testcontainers, has the
      real projector write rows into it and drives a KQL body through the same in-process pipeline
      every other test here uses, with the real ResourceGraphQueryService where the others have a
      recording fake. The pipeline is still served with no server and no socket; the container is
      the store behind the one seam whose whole worth is what a store answers — a fake ClickHouse
      would prove that the translator's SQL is the SQL the fake expects, which is the golden-file
      suite in CyberCloud.ResourceGraph.Tests and not a second thing worth proving. Like
      CyberCloud.ObjectStorage.Tests' SeaweedFS suite it needs a Docker daemon and fails without
      one rather than skipping: a gate that goes green when the store is absent is not a gate.
      ⚠ A SECOND ClickHouse SUITE SINCE #32, MonitorComponentViewsOverHttpTests, on the same terms and
      on a real socket (OverHttpGateway) — the reference that carries it is explained further down.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Shouldly"/>
        <PackageReference Include="NSubstitute"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Orleans.Multitenant"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Server"/>
        <!-- RedisHubTicketStoreTests. The same image and package every other Redis-backed suite uses. -->
        <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers.Redis"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="A real ClickHouse for the resource graph's end-to-end query — see the header">
        <PackageReference Include="Testcontainers"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      ⚠ THE SECOND SUITE THAT STEPS OUTSIDE THE NO-SILO SHAPE, AND WHY. KeyVaultOverTheGatewayTests
      drives the key vault's data plane (docs/plan/18) over HTTP through the nine stages into the REAL
      resource manager, the REAL ReBAC schema and the REAL KeyVaultGrain on an in-process silo, with
      a real OpenBao holding each vault's root. Every other suite here substitutes the manager; this
      one cannot, because what it asserts is who the real schema lets through — a control-plane owner
      refused, a data-plane role admitted, another tenant's caller answered 404 — and a substituted
      manager answers whatever its author believed. It lives here rather than in
      test/CyberCloud.Isolation because the stages are internal to the gateway and this is the one
      project they are visible to.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Label="An in-process silo and the key vault's independent verifier — see the comment above">
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.TestingHost"/>
        <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Orleans.Reminders"/>
        <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../CyberCloud.Authorization/CyberCloud.Authorization.csproj"/>
        <ProjectReference Include="../../CyberCloud.Identity/CyberCloud.Identity.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup Label="Global usings">
        <Using Include="Xunit"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <ItemGroup>
        <ProjectReference Include="../CyberCloud.Gateway.Host/CyberCloud.Gateway.Host.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      ⚠ THE MONITOR PROVIDER'S CONFORMANCE PROJECT, FOR ONE SUITE AND ONE REASON (#41).
      MonitorQueryOverHttpTests types queryMetrics, listMetricLabels and searchLogs at a real listener
      and needs them answered by the REAL write path — the action dispatcher, the permission check, the
      GUID the accountID and the database are derived from — which only an Orleans test cluster with
      the provider registered has. ProviderTestCluster<MonitorCase> is that cluster, built the way the
      provider's own conformance suite builds it, so the fixture composes a manager over it rather than
      a third copy of the harness. It brings a VictoriaMetrics cluster (three containers — the
      single-node image has no accountID) and a ClickHouse beside the one ResourceGraphQueryEndToEndTests
      starts; the header's "one container" is three suites now, and still none for the pipeline itself.

      ⚠ AND A SECOND SUITE OVER THE SAME PROJECT (#32). MonitorComponentViewsOverHttpTests (#32) asks whether another tenant's caller can read a
      component's telemetry over HTTP, and a recording manager cannot answer that: the refusal it must
      prove is the real manager's step 1 and the real handler never asking the store. The component's
      Docker-free conformance project carries ProviderTestCluster and the case — real grains, the real
      ResourceManagerService, the real action dispatcher — so it is referenced rather than rebuilt here.
      It needs a Docker daemon for its ClickHouse, like ResourceGraphQueryEndToEndTests.
    -->
    <ItemGroup Label="The Monitor provider's harness: the explorers' reads and the component views, over a real write path — see above">
        <ProjectReference Include="../../Providers/CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor/CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Conformance/CyberCloud.Providers.Monitor.Conformance.csproj"/>
    </ItemGroup>

</Project>
